+# MusicXML phrase-slur placement
+
+`Note.slurPlacement` and public `SlurPlacement` retain the notation side of an outgoing phrase slur. `Automatic` (0) preserves the existing stem-based layout; `Above` (1) and `Below` (2) request the corresponding side. Note cloning and Worker JSON retain this field. Older JSON without the field keeps `Automatic`.
+
+The MusicXML importer reads explicit `<slur placement="above|below">` on the start element. When the start omits placement, an explicit matching stop supplies the side. An explicit start remains authoritative when endpoint attributes disagree. The existing Note model still supports only one outgoing origin-to-destination phrase link. Two numbered outgoing slurs from a shared note head are not represented independently; SamePage issue #498 tracks this pre-existing limitation, with unchanged red controls on production and this fix. Do not duplicate source notes or change source sides to hide that limitation. This is one side for the existing origin-to-destination phrase model, including its continuation on later systems; it does not model an S-shaped slur with conflicting endpoint sides.
+
+The override belongs only to `ScoreSlurGlyph`. Pitch ties, note duration, MIDI generation, automatic unspecified slurs and effect-slur construction are unchanged. No source XML rewrite is required. Above/below placement differs from MusicXML `orientation` (overhand/underhand); custom orientation, continuation-specific shape and Bezier control-point overrides remain outside this change. Generic collision avoidance between an above phrase slur and another above notation is not provided by this side-retention fix.
+
+Regression tests: `test/rendering/MusicXmlSlurPlacement.test.ts` covers import, real native SVG geometry, public JSON, note clone, legacy JSON, Page/Horizontal, cross-system continuation, simultaneous voices, start/stop precedence and tie isolation. The original installed-package red repro and actual prepare/render Worker browser captures accompany SamePage issue #497.
+
+Reference: https://www.w3.org/2021/06/musicxml40/musicxml-reference/elements/slur/
+
+## Cross-staff phrase endpoints
+
+A stop first uses its existing staff-local numbered origin. If that staff has no origin, the importer accepts one unambiguous pending origin with the same number on another staff of the same part. This also permits an explicitly numbered voice handoff; voice identity is not a substitute for the source slur number. It never searches another part and does not choose between two pending origins on different staves. Existing same-staff behavior has precedence, including older files that reuse numbers on separate staves.
+
+This bounded correction covers a start preceding its stop in document order. It does not add arbitrary stop-before-start resolution, multiple outgoing slurs from one note, `continue` shape overrides or general cross-staff collision avoidance. Those are separate representation/layout boundaries. Model note links and their existing public Worker JSON remain the same; source notes and MIDI are not rewritten.
+
+`MusicXmlCrossStaffSlur.test.ts` covers both staff directions, explicit cross-voice numbering, mismatched-number and other-part controls, ambiguous fallback, and native Page/Horizontal rendering after public JSON. MusicXML number-level is defined per overlapping objects in a part, not as a staff identifier: https://www.w3.org/2021/06/musicxml40/musicxml-reference/data-types/number-level/ .
+
+For phrase slurs only, two staff renderers in the same staff system now form one continuous span. Pitch-tie and other glyph continuation rules retain their legacy staff comparison. Cross-staff phrase endpoints are translated between staff-local Y coordinates; their geometry is refreshed for paint after both staff positions have finalized. This avoids treating two staves of one system as two systems, while preserving actual system-break continuation. It is not a collision-avoidance algorithm.
+
+A same-system cross-staff phrase does not contribute the destination staff's whole vertical distance to its origin staff's overflow. Its automatic curve height is at least half the endpoint vertical separation, so a steep rising/falling phrase retains an outward bow instead of collapsing towards a diagonal straight line. This is an engraving default, not source Bezier reproduction or a proof that the curve avoids every intervening note. An owned regression compares complete SVG dimensions with the same notes without a slur to catch artificial inter-staff expansion.
+
+After all staves have finalized their vertical positions, the staff system measures registered cross-staff phrase ink and reserves only any excess outside the complete system as outer padding. This preserves the inter-staff distance while keeping outer above/below curves inside the SVG. The set is cleared and rebuilt on each system finalization. Owned tests exercise actual painted Bezier bounds, both outer sides, the first and last painted system-break segments with note anchors, and the unchanged legacy pitch-tie segmentation rule.
diff --git a/docs/musicxml-stem-visibility.md b/docs/musicxml-stem-visibility.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..77aaa09
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/musicxml-stem-visibility.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# MusicXML stem visibility
+
+`<stem>none</stem>` is retained as `Beat.isStemVisible = false` through generated cloning and public JSON/Worker transport. Missing fields in older JSON and other import formats default to `true`. Unknown stem values retain automatic engraving.
+
+The standard-notation renderer omits only the stem rectangle. Noteheads, written duration, MIDI, beam/flag/tuplet glyphs and the existing spacing/beam calculations are unchanged. This avoids converting stemless proportional/free notation into a different rhythm or silently removing separately printed rhythmic graphics. Layout can therefore retain normal stem clearance; this is not a new free-time spacing engine.
+
+The native beat represents one shared stem for its chord. An explicit `none` on any chord member suppresses that shared stem, without affecting other beats, voices or staves. It does not add independent conflicting stems inside one chord, cross-staff beam reconstruction, stem length controls or tablature/slash-staff stem support. Source files with unusual mixed-stem chord semantics need separate source adjudication.
+
+The owned tests compare actual SVG after public JSON, require only stem rectangles to disappear for quarter/half/eighth and chords/beamed groups, and retain all MIDI events and bar bounds. Actual source layouts and real browser Workers remain release gates.
diff --git a/docs/musicxml-timed-staff-notation.md b/docs/musicxml-timed-staff-notation.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f1046c0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/musicxml-timed-staff-notation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# MusicXML timed staff notation prototype
+
+MusicXML attributes apply in musical score order, including when another voice is
+read after a backup. A bar's existing `clef`, `clefOttava`, `keySignature` and
+`keySignatureType` remain its initial context. `Bar.notationChanges` holds later
+changes in bar-local display ticks (960 ticks per quarter), including the bar end.
+The next bar inherits the final context. JSON/Worker serialization preserves the
+list; omitted lists retain the previous whole-bar behavior.
+
+Standard notation resolves each head's staff position and accidental context at
+its own beat time. Clef changes retain accidental carry by written pitch; key
+changes start a new accidental scope. The renderer reserves an independent
+spacing spring for a change inside a held note. No musical beat, rest, duration,
+pitch, or playback event is added. Bar-end symbols occupy the pre-barline area.
+
+The current timed import scope is pitched G2, F4, C3 and C4 clefs, octave changes
+-2 through 2, traditional integer fifths -7 through 7, numbered staff keys or
+unnumbered keys on every declared staff, explicit key cancellation, and
+`print-object="no"` for a timed glyph. Hidden changes still change the subsequent
+notation context. Additional/cue clefs, other clef lines, TAB and percussion
+changes, and nontraditional key signatures are conservatively left unsupported;
+they must not turn into an invented default treble clef. This does not add timed
+meter/transpose/staff-detail support. Numbered-notation key labels and custom
+engraving size/position attributes remain outside this standard-staff prototype.
+
+The owned tests use inline MusicXML, real Page/Horizontal SVG, JSON round trips,
+and every MIDI field/bar bound. Copyrighted score evidence is kept outside the
+repository. Actual Arirang checks additionally cover end-of-bar 8/9 clefs,
+bar 29's internal clef change, and bar 50's end-key cancellation. A model round
+trip is not by itself evidence that a real browser renderer Worker loaded it;
+the latter is a separate integration gate before packaging.
+
+Primary references:
+- https://www.w3.org/2021/06/musicxml40/musicxml-reference/elements/attributes/
+- https://www.w3.org/2021/06/musicxml40/musicxml-reference/elements/clef/
+- https://www.w3.org/2021/06/musicxml40/musicxml-reference/elements/key/
diff --git a/docs/musicxml-tuplet-display.md b/docs/musicxml-tuplet-display.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..14862e2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/musicxml-tuplet-display.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# MusicXML tuplet display
+
+An explicit `notations/tuplet` start can set `show-number="none|actual|both"` and `bracket="yes|no"`. These affect display only. `time-modification` continues to determine note durations, MIDI, and the common timeline.
+
+The imported declaration belongs to the beat starting the rendered tuplet group. The optional `Beat.sourceTupletNumber` and `Beat.sourceTupletBracket` fields survive generated clones and the public JSON/Worker boundary. Missing declarations and old JSON retain automatic engraving. Unknown attribute values are ignored. The display flags never create a tuplet or change its ratio.
+
+For a complete native tuplet group, `none` suppresses the number, `actual` shows the numerator, and `both` shows the ratio. `bracket="no"` suppresses the bracket independently. A bracket with no number has no artificial gap. Existing automatic grouping remains in place; this does not add MusicXML nested-span, cross-bar, separate placement, show-type, or custom tuplet-number support. Explicit bracket requests on incomplete native groups retain that renderer's existing number-only fallback.
+
+Source transcription must distinguish printed tuplets from ratios introduced for a deterministic practice grid. Only hide an editorial ratio when the original score and the recorded editorial policy support it. Do not remove timing ratios or hide genuine printed tuplets to make a screenshot match.
+
+The regression fixture exercises both flags separately and together, explicit ratio display, a following ordinary group, public JSON roundtrip, and exact MIDI/boundary preservation. Recheck actual source scores in both Page and Horizontal layouts before packaging a release.
diff --git a/docs/samepage-musicxml-cues.md b/docs/samepage-musicxml-cues.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9987214
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/samepage-musicxml-cues.md
@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+# Same Page MusicXML cue and hidden-rest support
+
+This patch retains timed pitched `<cue/>` notes as `Note.isCue` in the model.
+They keep ordinary score time and engraving, but generate no note or note-effect
+MIDI. `<type size="cue">` controls small notation independently: a small note
+without `<cue/>` still sounds, and a cue with `size="full"` remains silent.
+`Note.isSmall`, `Note.isCue`, and `Beat.isRestVisible` survive JSON serialization
+and cloning; omitted fields keep the previous ordinary-note/visible-rest defaults.
+Visual ties to/from cues remain available to engraving, but cannot extend an
+ordinary sounding note or suppress its attack.
+
+A rest note with `print-object="no"` keeps rhythmic layout, duration, navigation,
+and any separate direction marks. Only its rest/dot ink is suppressed, including
+slash rendering. A hidden rest cannot hide a coincident visible rest. The source
+MusicXML is never rewritten by this native importer. A wrapper may separately
+normalize `<forward>` to an invisible rest; this module does not invent source
+rest content or interpret a source annotation as a mandatory alternate passage.
+
+Timed small heads participate in shared multi-voice layout. Each head's own scale
+is used for its SMuFL stem attachment. Opposing-stem small/ordinary heads must have
+separate ink. Same-direction heads may meet only inside the font's shared-stem
+attachment band; requiring disjoint bounding rectangles (or disjoint ink even at
+that common stem) is an overly strong engraving criterion. Regression tests use
+the repository's Bravura font, actual painted SVG positions, isolated raster ink,
+and an intentionally coincident control. No arbitrary spacing gap is introduced.
+
+Scope: this is pitched timed-cue and hidden-rest support, not a general cue-layout
+engine. Cue boxes, source-relative x/y offsets, arbitrary optional-performance
+semantics, cue-rest sizing, and orchestral cue-label conventions are not recreated.
+The two-note boxed reference in Zhou piano 22 is a separately documented editorial
+silent display choice, not evidence that these notes must be played.
